{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Warren1968-T3-HLLH-x2",
  "Source": "S. W. Warren, \"Properties and performance of basic designs of infrared interference filters,\" Infrared Phys. 8, 65-78 (1968). DOI not printed in the paper - Table 3 p. 71 and Table 4 p. 73, first row; footnote \"HLLHS written in full would be HLLHLHLLHS\"",
  "Notes": "Double half-wave bandpass from basic unit HLLHS (written in full HLLHLHLLHS), 7 layers\nSpecification: Wide bandpass at lambda0.\nReported: Table 3: bandwidth (no errors) 11.6 % of peak wavelength; half-wave layer error of 13.8 % / 7.5 % reduces peak transmission by factor 0.5 / 0.7. Table 4: T at lambda0/lambda = 1 = 84.6 % without absorption (recomputed 84.59 % with incoherent back surface).\nNotes: Design string written out: HLLHLHLLHS. H = quarter-wave optical thickness of index 4.0, L = quarter wave of index 1.8 at lambda0, S = substrate of index 1.65 (p. 66); adjacent equal letters (HH, LL) are merged into one half-wave layer of 2 QWOT. The paper writes designs from the incident (air) side to S, so the order is kept as printed. lambda0 is not fixed for Tables 1-3 and the figures (results are in lambda0/lambda); 4000 nm (4 um, as in Tables 4-6) is used, which is harmless because all indices are constant. Recomputations against the paper match only if the uncoated back surface of the n=1.65 substrate is included incoherently (substrate -> air): air/L/1.65 alone gives T = 89.4 % at lambda0 while Table 4 prints 84.6 %, and with the back surface it is 84.6 %; the rejection minima of Sec. 2.1.3 (3.45 %, 0.087 %, 0.0020 %, 0.000047 %) also match exactly only with the back surface. Normal incidence, unpolarised.\nLayers from Table 3 p. 71 and Table 4 p. 73, first row; footnote \"HLLHS written in full would be HLLHLHLLHS\"; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours T 84.6 % at 4 um; paper T 84.6 % (Table 4).",
